        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b43776fb-5a83-56d7-ba16-d87b699f97e8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: George Lashwood, Beau Brummell, Edward George Wright, George Bryan Brummell, Prince Regent, King George IV\n- Roles: English singer, fashion arbiter\n- Places: England, London's East End, Blackpool\n- Eras: Edwardian era, Regency England\n- Topics: music halls, men's fashion\n\nFacts:\n- George Bryan \"Beau\" Brummell was born on 7 June 1778 and died on 30 March 1840.\n- Beau Brummell was an iconic figure in Regency England, an arbiter of men's fashion, and a friend of the Prince Regent, who later became King George IV.\n- George Lashwood was born Edward George Wright on 25 April 1863 and died on 20 January 1942.\n- George Lashwood was a popular English singer of the Edwardian era who performed in music halls across the country, especially in London's East End and at seaside locations such as Blackpool.\n- George Lashwood was known as \"the Beau Brummell of the music halls.\"",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
